    <body>
      <sec id="article-31335.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Wernicke aphasia (WA) represents a fluent aphasia characterized by markedly impaired auditory&#x02013;verbal comprehension with relatively preserved rate, prosody, and grammar but frequent semantic and phonemic paraphasias, neologisms, and jargon. The lesion classically localizes to the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere and most commonly follows acute ischemic stroke involving the inferior division of the middle cerebral artery. Reading and writing often mirror spoken-language deficits; repetition is typically impaired, and anosognosia is frequently observed. Bedside evaluation assesses naming, fluency, comprehension of single-step and multistep commands, repetition, reading, and writing; formal tools (eg, the Boston Diagnostic Aphasia Examination) and neuroimaging help confirm the diagnosis and exclude mimics such as delirium or Alzheimer disease. Recovery typically peaks within 2 to 6 months after the stroke, depending on lesion location and size, patient age, and the integrity of contralateral networks. Extension into the middle or inferior temporal gyri or the inferior parietal lobule often portends poorer outcomes.</p>
        <p>This course equips participants to recognize WA rapidly, perform a structured language examination, select appropriate imaging and neuropsychological testing, and initiate evidence-informed communication strategies. Instruction emphasizes secondary stroke prevention and medication optimization in coordination with a pharmacist and tailored speech-language therapy planning with a speech-language pathologist, neuropsychologist, and neurologist. Collaboration with occupational and physical therapists supports functional recovery and safety; nursing, case management, and social work reinforce caregiver education and community resources. An interprofessional approach aligns goals across disciplines, reduces communication barriers, improves adherence to rehabilitation, and measurably enhances patient-centered outcomes, including participation, quality of life, and reintegration into daily activities.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Create individualized communication strategies incorporating visual aids, caregiver training, and simplified language techniques.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate WA from common mimics (eg, acute delirium, psychogenic disorders, Alzheimer disease) by applying a structured bedside language examination and mental status assessment.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate the effectiveness of therapy interventions through outcome measures such as language function scales and quality-of-life indices.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement interprofessional communication and coordinated care plans with speech-language pathologists, neuropsychologists, pharmacists, occupational/physical therapists, nursing, and social work to optimize rehabilitation and secondary stroke prevention.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=31335&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=31335">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Aphasia is an impairment of language function localized to the perisylvian area of the dominant cerebral hemisphere. Carl Wernicke and Ludwig Lichtheim developed the classical model of aphasia in the 19th century, which was&#x000a0;further refined neuroanatomically by Norman Geschwind in the 1960s.&#x000a0;This model provides the foundation for understanding the clinical features of aphasia and the corresponding neuroanatomical lesions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;According to this model, language&#x000a0;processing begins with&#x000a0;comprehension in the Wernicke area, located in the posterior section of the superior temporal gyrus, Brodmann area 22. Information is then conveyed through the arcuate fasciculus to the Broca area in the inferior frontal gyrus, responsible for language output or expression. See<bold> Image.</bold> The Broca and Wernicke Areas of the Brain.</p>
        <p>Wernicke aphasia (WA) is characterized by impaired language comprehension, with fluent yet nonsensical speech.&#x000a0;Naming, repetition, reading, and writing are also affected. Despite impaired comprehension, speech may have a normal rate, rhythm, and grammar. Patients with WA have impaired speech comprehension and thus do not recognize their speech errors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The most common cause of&#x000a0;WA is an ischemic stroke affecting the posteroinferior division of the middle cerebral artery of the dominant hemisphere.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The most common cause of&#x000a0;WA is ischemic stroke affecting the superior temporal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere's posterior temporal lobe. Other causes of WA are brain injury, primary or metastatic tumors, central nervous system infections, and&#x000a0;neurodegenerative&#x000a0;disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;WA often results from an embolic stroke that affects the posteroinferior division of the dominant middle cerebral artery, which supplies the temporal cortex.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Data on the incidence of WA are limited. Annually, approximately 170,000 new cases of aphasia are&#x000a0;associated with&#x000a0;stroke.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;In a population-based study from Zurich, Switzerland, results showed that 43 per 100,000 residents developed aphasia per year due to a first acute ischemic stroke, predominantly from a cardioembolic source.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from another more recent population-based study from Lund, Sweden, revealed an incidence of aphasia of 31 per 100,000 person-years, and 30% of patients with ischemic stroke&#x000a0;presented with aphasia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Carl Wernicke and Ludwig Lichtheim developed the classical model of aphasia in the 19th century, which was further refined neuroanatomically by Normand Geschwind in the 1960s.&#x000a0;This model provides the foundation for understanding the clinical features of aphasia and the corresponding neuroanatomical lesions. The language centers are typically located in the dominant hemisphere, most often the left, within the perisylvian region. Spoken language&#x000a0;input is received by the primary auditory cortices in the Heschl (transverse temporal gyrus) and processed in the Wernicke area. The Wernicke area is located in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the dominant cerebral hemisphere (Broca&#x000a0;area 22). This area is closely associated with the auditory cortex. Language function localizes to the left cerebral hemisphere in almost all right-handed individuals and approximately 60% of left-handed individuals. Written language is transmitted from the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe to the angular gyrus and then to the Wernicke area.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>The contemporary language model, also known as the dual-stream model, was developed by Gregory Hickok and David Poeppel and is supported by neuroimaging studies, including functional MRI, MRI diffusion tensor imaging, and MRI tractography. The dual-stream model&#x000a0;posits 2 primary language processing streams, involving both cortical and subcortical structures. The dorsal stream is located in the gray matter of the frontoparietal regions of the dominant hemisphere. This structure processes auditory-to-articulation information, connecting the frontal speech areas and the temporoparietal junction. This stream supports&#x000a0;fluent speech production. The ventral stream, located in both temporal lobes, processes auditory-to-meaning information, essential for auditory comprehension. This stream encompasses much of the gray matter in the lateral temporal lobe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Regardless of the different language models, the classical neuroanatomical model, which posits a structural lesion in the Wernicke area as the cause of WA, remains the most&#x000a0;practical model for localizing structural lesions in clinical practice.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>In Wernicke aphasia, language output is fluent with a normal rate and prosody. However, the content is often difficult to understand because of paraphasic errors. Paraphasias are typically semantic (one word is substituted for another) or phonemic (one sound or syllable is substituted for another). For example, a patient may say "watch" instead of "clock" in the case of a semantic paraphasic error. An example of a phonemic paraphasic error would be a patient saying "dock" instead of "clock." In severe cases, these errors can result in neologisms (new words) or "word salad" (an unintelligible mixture of words and phrases), which makes communication nearly impossible. Due to these deficits, patients may find it easier to substitute a generic word, such as "thing" or "stuff," rather than saying the intended word. Reading comprehension is also often impaired in WA. As with Broca aphasia, repetition is also impaired.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Unlike Broca aphasia, patients with WA speak with normal fluency and prosody and follow grammatical rules with normal sentence structure.&#x000a0;Associated neurological symptoms depend on the size and location of the lesion and include&#x000a0;visual field deficits, trouble with calculation (acalculia), and writing (agraphia). An early clue to WA is abnormal spelling. As opposed to Broca aphasia, patients with&#x000a0;WA often do not have hemiparesis accompanying the language deficit; they also do not display the same degree of emotional outbursts and depression seen with Broca aphasia. In general, patients with WA are not aware of their deficits (anosognosia) and may become frustrated when others are not able to understand them. Sometimes, patients may become aware of errors in language if it is presented to them in an audio format.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Each language component should be tested upon clinical examination, including verbal fluency, object naming, repeating simple phrases, comprehension of simple and complex commands, reading, and writing. When testing comprehension, it is advisable to start with simple commands such as "close your eyes" or "open your mouth." Then, progress to more complex commands, such as "show me two fingers on your left hand," and commands that require crossing the midline, like "touch your right ear with your left hand." The Boston Diagnostic Aphasia Examination is a widely used test to evaluate patients with language deficits.&#x000a0;Formal neuropsychological testing&#x000a0;may be necessary to&#x000a0;determine&#x000a0;the type and severity of the language deficit. Neuroimaging (CT, MRI, functional MRI, positron emission tomography, or single photon emission computed tomography) may be required to localize lesions and determine the etiology of the aphasia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;A recently described diagnostic framework that analyzes topological changes in resting-state functional MRI-derived functional brain networks&#x000a0;enhances the diagnostic accuracy of specific language deficits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Wernicke aphasia must be distinguished from Alzheimer disease. In both conditions, patients may struggle to answer basic orientation questions. In WA, the primary deficit is comprehension, whereas in dementia, the primary issue is memory loss. Alzheimer disease tends to be subacute in onset and progressive in nature, as opposed to WA, which is sudden in onset due to ischemic stroke. Neuroimaging of the brain&#x000a0;helps distinguish between these 2 conditions.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Currently, there is no&#x000a0;established disease-modifying therapy for WA.&#x000a0;Speech and language therapy is the primary treatment for patients with aphasia. Because comprehension is impaired, patients may lack insight into their deficits, making remediation challenging. An interdisciplinary care plan, developed with a speech-language pathologist, neuropsychologist, and neurologist, helps optimize&#x000a0;patient outcomes. The treatment plan focuses on maximizing residual language abilities, enhancing language skills, and developing alternative communication strategies to support effective communication. Furthermore, group therapy allows patients to practice communication skills and reduce social isolation. Many commercial software programs claim that&#x000a0;their products will improve language function. However, most of these products have not undergone rigorous clinical trials.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Because different&#x000a0;types of language dysfunction arise in distinct neural regions, treatment strategies that target a single neural region for aphasia&#x000a0;may be ineffective or even impair recovery, depending on the specific language profile of the individual.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Researchers are conducting clinical trials to evaluate medical treatments for aphasia. Pharmacologic therapies under investigation include&#x000a0;catecholaminergic agents (bromocriptine, levodopa, amantadine, dextroamphetamine), nootropic drugs such as piracetam, and medications used to treat Alzheimer disease such as donepezil and memantine. Nonpharmacologic approaches include transcranial magnetic stimulation and transcranial direct current stimulation. Trials to date have been small with mixed results, and further studies are needed to determine the effectiveness of these approaches.&#x000a0;Improvements in aphasia can occur long after a stroke, so efforts should focus on optimizing communication. Notably, family and social support are crucial for achieving a positive outcome. Treatment of poststroke depression and cognitive issues, as well as&#x000a0;remediation of other neurological disorders such as neglect, agnosia, and hemiparesis, should be addressed during rehabilitation to optimize patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Wernicke aphasia is most commonly caused by an acute ischemic stroke affecting the posteroinferior division of the dominant middle cerebral artery. Other pathologies affecting the dominant Wernicke area include primary or metastatic tumors, traumatic brain injury, neurodegenerative disorders (including Alzheimer disease), and primary progressive aphasia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r17">[17]</xref> Patients with WA present with&#x000a0;impaired comprehension and&#x000a0;fluent speech with paraphasic errors. As a result, these patients may be labeled "confused" with the common differential diagnosis of acute delirium. Patients with acute delirium have intact language but impaired sensorium and attention.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>When WA is due to a stroke, recovery of language function peaks within 2 to 6 months, after which further progress is typically limited. Most patients exhibit rapid improvement within the first 2 to 3 months, with&#x000a0;slower improvement thereafter. Language deficits improve at a similar pace. The mechanisms&#x000a0;of the rapid recovery may include recruitment or reorganization of ipsilateral perilesional tissue or homotopic regions in the&#x000a0;nondominant hemisphere.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r18">[18]</xref> WA usually involves the posterior third of the superior temporal gyrus. Involvement of the middle or inferior temporal gyri or the inferior parietal lobule makes recovery unlikely. Recovery also depends on the location and size, patient age, and integrity of the contralateral cortex.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The complications of WA are determined primarily by the underlying cause and the associated neuroanatomic deficits. For example, impaired comprehension often results in frustration and depression. Patients may have difficulty expressing needs, which can impede recovery and rehabilitation. Involvement of adjacent structures may also produce deficits in visuospatial perception and working memory.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>The&#x000a0;most common cause of WA is acute ischemic stroke. The most crucial aspect of patient and family education, in addition to speech-language therapy and stroke rehabilitation, is secondary stroke prevention. Patients should be counseled on lifestyle modifications such as a healthy diet, control of hypertension and blood glucose, smoking cessation, statin therapy, and antiplatelet therapy.&#x000a0;If the patient has atrial fibrillation or other causes of cardioembolic stroke, long-term anticoagulation&#x000a0;is indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s13" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>When communicating with patients with WA, it is essential to eliminate background noise and distractions, maintain eye contact, and&#x000a0;speak at a normal volume and rate. Ideally, sentences should be short, and one should avoid asking questions that require a lengthy explanation. Yes-or-no questions are often preferable. Patients may prefer to write their answers, so it is advisable to keep a notepad and pen available.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31335.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>WA is often challenging to diagnose. Patients may&#x000a0;speak fluently with grammatically correct sentences at a normal rate, but their speech frequently lacks meaningful content. They may use many irrelevant words and lack awareness of a speech deficit. Due to this lack of insight, these patients may&#x000a0;present with unrelated concerns, and&#x000a0;clinicians need to be aware of the signs of WA. While there is no specific therapy, recent studies suggest that a focus on communication and functional treatment may be beneficial for some individuals. The prognosis&#x000a0;is strongly dependent on the primary condition causing WA. Recovery can take months or years, and some patients have adverse long-term outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31335.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Clinicians&#x000a0;should collaborate with&#x000a0;other members of the interprofessional team, including pharmacists, speech-language therapists, occupational therapists, and other specialists, to optimize patient outcomes and improve speech parameters. Pharmacists can help review medications and interactions. Speech-language and occupational therapists can treat aphasia and help individuals develop effective communication skills. Clinicians in other specialties may recognize WA during encounters for other patient concerns. Neurologists can assist the interdisciplinary team with treatment planning.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
